<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        Track LTI initialisation separately from `activated`.

        The LTI launch flow used `activated` as its "has this user already been shown their Artemis password"
        marker: the launch created the account unactivated, buildLtiResponse added ?initialize because of that,
        and the initialisation endpoint returned a generated password and set activated = true, so later launches
        skipped the dialog.

        That overloaded a flag whose only meaning is "may authenticate". An LTI account is provisioned by the
        launch rather than registered by its owner, so it has to be created activated - which leaves the flow
        without a marker of its own. This column is that marker.

        Backfill: an LTI account that is already activated has been through the dialog, so it is marked
        initialised. One that is not activated has either never been initialised or was deactivated by an
        administrator; either way it keeps the default of false, so a genuinely uninitialised account still gets
        its dialog, while a deactivated one is no longer activated by it.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260820105243-01-add-lti-initialized" author="krusche">
        <addColumn tableName="jhi_user">
            <column name="lti_initialized" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="false">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260820105243-02-backfill-lti-initialized" author="krusche">
        <update tableName="jhi_user">
            <column name="lti_initialized" valueBoolean="true"/>
            <where>lti_created = true AND activated = true</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>
    <!--
        Recover an LTI account whose first launch never finished.

        Under the old flow the launch created the account unactivated and the initialisation dialog activated it, so an
        account whose owner closed the launch before that dialog completed stayed unactivated. It could still launch again,
        because the launch did not check account state. It does now, which would leave exactly this population unable to
        launch at all - so the state has to be repaired in the same migration that introduces the marker.

        `last_modified_by` separates the two ways an LTI account can be unactivated. Deactivating an account is an
        authenticated administrative action, so it stamps the administrator's login; an account that never got past
        provisioning has never been written by an authenticated principal and still carries the unauthenticated default. An
        account an administrator deactivated can therefore not match this predicate, which is what keeps that decision
        intact. Soft-deleted rows are excluded outright.

        `lti_initialized` is deliberately left false for these rows: they are activated so the launch works, and the dialog
        is still owed, which is exactly the state the old flow would have left them in.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260820105243-03-recover-unfinished-lti-provisioning" author="krusche">
        <update tableName="jhi_user">
            <column name="activated" valueBoolean="true"/>
            <where>lti_created = true AND activated = false AND is_deleted = false AND (last_modified_by IS NULL OR last_modified_by IN ('system', 'anonymousUser'))</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
